 ## 中文摘要
 
-任务图演化能够组织新增义务，但不能直接代表产物继承或验收改善。本文结合Agent Center的195个非显式夹具generation快照、真实案例及256次受控短题响应，区分任务图、候选产物、合同和环境。历史证据同时包含图保留而能力缺失、不同提交同完整树、原任务续修与后继合同替代，说明单看代际不足以判断交付。实验交叉累计／活动图及产物、合同关系联表／分表，两模型原严格得分均96/128，但候选与合同字段均128/128；全部负分来自两题的动作分类歧义，不能用于DAG负面归因。排除两题全部条件后，两模型均96/96，各因素配对差均0；另冻结64次材料修复复测，单独保存。本文提出以变化前提、仍有效成果和待满足合同约束恢复形式。当前证据未证明动态图净收益、长期成本或内部认知原因，全部分析尚未人工复核。
+任务失败或需求变化后，动态任务图可以重组工作，但新增代际容易与代码修复、成果继承和验收改善混为一谈。本文研究DAG演化实际解决什么问题，以及何时应改图、续修、补证或等待。采用单部署机制案例分析，结合Agent Center的195个非显式夹具generation快照、169个父子转移、产物与合同谱系及256次合成处置响应。案例表明，局部改图用于安排新增复验和交付义务，而既有任务也可以直接续修；一项修复报告甚至早于对应改图。图中保留历史节点不保证后继代码保留成果，不同提交也可能具有相同完整树；新合同下的通过不能追认已撤回的旧结论。结构统计中149/169次转移保留全部父节点，反映历史累积而非交付收益。受控实验的六个有效情境中，两模型均96/96正确，表明在当前事实充分且规则明确时，累计图与活动图、联表与分表都可支持正确处置。本文据此将DAG演化定位为工作组织的适应机制：改图应对应真实义务或依赖的变化，产物继承和验收有效性应分别核验。研究未估计动态策略的净收益，全部分析尚未人工复核。
 
 **关键词：**DAG演化；Agent harness；产物继承；验收合同；过程重建
 
 ## Abstract
 
-Task-graph evolution can organize new obligations but does not directly establish artifact inheritance or improved acceptance. This study combines 195 Agent Center generation snapshots after excluding explicit fixture plans, historical cases, and 256 controlled short-task responses. It separates the task graph, candidate artifact, contract, and environment. Historical evidence includes preserved graph history with missing capabilities, different commits with identical complete trees, in-task repair, and replacement contracts. The experiment crossed cumulative versus active graph views with joined versus distributed artifact and contract relations. Both models scored 96/128 under the frozen strict rubric, although candidate and contract fields were correct in all 128 responses. Every mismatch came from overlapping action categories in two fixtures and is excluded from negative DAG attribution. Across the other six scenarios, both models scored 96/96 with zero paired differences for all factors. A separately frozen 64-call material-repair study is preserved independently. The analysis motivates recovery choices grounded in changed premises, retained artifacts, and outstanding contracts, without establishing the net delivery value of dynamic graphs, long-term cost, or internal cognitive causes. Human verification has not been completed.
+After task failure or a change in requirements, dynamic task graphs can reorganize work, but new generations are easily conflated with code repair, artifact inheritance, and improved acceptance. This study asks what DAG evolution actually accomplishes and when to edit a graph, continue an existing task, add evidence, or wait. A single-deployment mechanism analysis combines 195 Agent Center generation snapshots after explicit fixture exclusion, 169 parent–child transitions, artifact and contract lineages, and 256 synthetic decision responses. Cases show local edits organizing additional validation and delivery obligations, while other defects are repaired within existing tasks; one repair report precedes its corresponding graph edit. Retained graph nodes do not guarantee retained code capabilities, different commits may have identical complete trees, and acceptance under a new contract does not reinstate a withdrawn earlier verdict. Of 169 transitions, 149 retain all parent nodes, describing historical accumulation rather than delivery benefit. In the six valid experimental scenarios, both models score 96/96 across cumulative versus active views and joined versus distributed relations. Under explicit rules and sufficient facts, either representation supports correct decisions. We therefore characterize DAG evolution as adaptation of work organization: graph changes should correspond to changed obligations or dependencies, while artifact inheritance and acceptance validity require separate checks. Net delivery gains from dynamic strategies remain unmeasured, and human verification is outstanding.
 
 **Keywords:** DAG evolution; agent harness; artifact inheritance; acceptance contract; process reconstruction
 
